    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness.jsonld#process/Process_RefreshMaterialized",
      "@type": "bootstrap:Process",
      "conformsTo": [
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness.jsonld#externalSchema/omg-bpmn-2.0",
        "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness.jsonld#externalSchema/omg-dd-1.0"
      ],
      "depiction": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/docs/cat-harness/assets/img/workflows/refresh-materialized.svg",
      "description": "Keep a materialized copy of remote content current: what changed upstream, what changed locally, and what to do when both did — an archival copy is never re-fetched and gets a fixity check instead. Owner, 2026-09-20: \"also need to know about refreshing amterialed remote content. general process used everywhere.\"\n\nREFRESH IS NOT RE-IMPORT, and the difference is the whole diagram. It needs three answers, not one: what changed upstream, what changed locally since, and what to do when BOTH did. upstream-pins.json answers the first for one caller and nothing answers the other two anywhere.\n\nAn ARCHIVAL copy is not refreshed. Re-fetching it would defeat it: the copy exists to survive the source, so replacing it with whatever the source says today discards exactly the state it was keeping. What an archive gets instead is a FIXITY CHECK — has our copy rotted — which is a different question with a different remedy.",
      "enforcement": "strict",
      "flowCount": 17,
      "inSubgraph": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness.jsonld#directory/processes",
      "name": "Refresh materialized remote content",
      "nodeCount": 14,
      "sourcePath": "processes/library/refresh-materialized.bpmn",
      "sourceUrl": "https://github.com/litlfred/folio-assistant/blob/main/cat-harness/processes/library/refresh-materialized.bpmn",
      "startNode": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness.jsonld#process/Process_RefreshMaterialized/node/StartEvent_Due",
      "summary": "Keep a materialized copy of remote content current: what changed upstream, what changed locally, and what to do when both did — an archival copy is never re-fetched and gets a fixity check instead."
    },
